        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="16" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=20;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#6C8EBF;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=10;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="9" target="11">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="17" value="Both commands share the same primitives — safeRoot (os.Root confinement, symlink refused in EVERY path component), atomic write (temp file + fsync + rename, existing mode preserved) and an ownership manifest with SHA-256 per artifact: commit on success, rollback on any failure. install refuses to run as root and resolves KIRO_HOME (must be absolute, defaults to ~/.kiro). init requires the binary to already resolve — KIRO_SECOPS_BIN → ~/.kiro/bin/kiro-secops → own executable → PATH — and writes the validated absolute path into every hook command, so a PATH hijack cannot swap the guard. Because that path is machine-local, whoever clones the repo must run install + init again: 'kiro-secops verify --project .' reports BROKEN for hooks pinned to another machine (the guard would be silently OFF). 'kiro-secops uninstall' restores exactly the state recorded in the manifest." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F5F5F5;strokeColor=#666666;dashed=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=8;spacingRight=10;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="530" width="1080" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
